    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Tower weights `W\u2096\u208a\u2081 = 2^W\u2096 \u00b7 W\u2096`: what is now proved, and what remains\n\nTwo self-contained Lean files carry the development:\n\n* `Catalog/Pythagorean/TowerRadix.lean` \u2014 the tower weights `W`, the position function `K`,\n  the tower radix representation, binary encoding bounds, the iterated logarithm `L\u2082`,\n  the Zeckendorf comparison, and the radix-growth threshold;\n* `Catalog/Pythagorean/MixedRadix.lean` \u2014 general mixed radix systems (standard and\n  balanced), and the balanced tower digits.\n\nEverything below is a theorem in those files unless explicitly flagged as open.  No file\ncontains `sorry`, and the main results use only Lean's standard axioms.\n\n## Results\n\n### 0. The basic identity\n\n`W k = 2 ^ (\u2211_{i<k} W i)` (`W_eq_two_pow_sumW`): each tower weight is a power of two whose\nexponent is the sum of the previous weights.  Almost every later result rests on this.\n\n### 1. Iterated-log position bound (conjectured, now proved)\n\nWith `L\u2082 n` the number of iterations of `x \u21a6 \u2308log\u2082(x+1)\u2309` needed to reach a value `\u2264 2`:\n\n* `K_le_L2_add_two`: `K n \u2264 L\u2082 n + 2`, via the one-step inequality `K n \u2264 K (clg n) + 1`;\n* `L2_le_K_add_one`: `L\u2082 n \u2264 K n + 1`, via the auxiliary estimate that everything below\n  `2\u00b7\u2211_{i<k} W\u1d62` is killed by `k` iterations;\n* `abs_K_sub_L2_le_two`: `|K n \u2212 L\u2082 n| \u2264 2` for **every** `n` (the conjecture asked for\n  `n \u2265 2`).\n\nThe asymmetric shape is sharp: `K \u2212 L\u2082 = 2` occurs at `n = 2`, and `K \u2212 L\u2082 \u2265 \u22121` always.\n\n### 2. No worst-case binary compression (conjectured, now proved)\n\n* `no_binary_compression`: any encoding injective on `[0, W\u2096)` has a codeword of length at\n  least `\u2211_{i<k} W\u1d62 = log\u2082 W\u2096`.  This is stronger than the conjecture, which only asked\n  for prefix-free codes; the reason the stronger form holds is that `W\u2096` is exactly a\n  power of two.\n* `no_binary_compression_digits`: the same statement phrased for the valid length-`k` digit\n  families.\n* `blockCode_optimal`: writing digit `i` in exactly `W\u1d62` bits and concatenating gives an\n  injective code whose codewords all have length exactly `\u2211_{i<k} W\u1d62`, so the lower bound\n  is attained.\n\n### 3. Radix-growth threshold (conjectured, proved in the following form)\n\nFor generalised weights `V\u2080 = 1`, `V\u2096\u208a\u2081 = r(V\u2096)\u00b7V\u2096`:\n\n* `genPos_le_L2_add_two`: if `r x \u2265 2^x` for all `x`, then the position function satisfies\n  `genPos n \u2264 L\u2082 n + 2`, i.e. it is `O(log* n)`.  Monotonicity of `r`, assumed in the\n  informal conjecture, turned out to be unnecessary.\n* `sqPos_not_bigO_L2`: for the borderline polynomial radix `r x = x`, i.e.\n  `V\u2096 = 2^(2^k)`, the position function is *not* `O(L\u2082)`: for every `c` there is an `n`\n  with `c \u00b7 L\u2082 n < sqPos n`.\n\nThe remaining gap: the negative half is proved for the concrete family `V\u2096 = 2^(2^k)`\nrather than for every `r` with `r x \u2264 x^C`.  The general case should follow from\n`V\u2096 \u2264 V\u2080^(C^k)` by the same argument, but the bookkeeping with the parameters `C` and\n`V\u2080` has not been carried out.\n\n### 4. Balanced recursive digits (conjectured, now proved)\n\n`MixedRadix` develops mixed radix systems for an arbitrary radix sequence: existence and\nuniqueness of digits (`digit_sum`, `digit_unique`), and, for odd radices\n`r\u1d62 = 2s\u1d62 + 1`, existence and uniqueness of *balanced* digits (`balanced_exists`,\n`balanced_unique`).  Specialising to `r\u1d62 = 2^(W\u1d62+1)+1` gives\n`TowerBalanced.balanced_tower_repr`: every integer `x` with `2|x| + 1 \u2264 U\u2096` has a unique\nlength-`k` expansion with `i`-th digit in `[\u22122^W\u1d62, 2^W\u1d62] = [\u2212(r\u1d62\u22121)/2, (r\u1d62\u22121)/2]`.\n\n### 5. Zeckendorf comparison (partly proved, one clause corrected)\n\n* `Zidx_bounds`: for `n \u2265 1`, `log\u2082 n + 1 \u2264 Z(n) \u2264 2 log\u2082 n + 3`, with `Z(n)` the largest\n  Fibonacci index with `F_{Z(n)} \u2264 n` (the top index of the canonical Zeckendorf\n  expansion).  This realises the conjectured constants `a`, `b` explicitly as `1` and `2`\n  (plus additive slack).\n* `K_littleO_Zidx`: `K n = o(Z n)`, in the form: for every `c` there is `N` with\n  `c \u00b7 K n \u2264 Z n` for all `n \u2265 N`.\n* The final clause of the conjecture \u2014 that *every injective* binary encoding has\n  worst-case length at least `\u2308log\u2082(n+1)\u2309` on `[0, n]` \u2014 is **false** as stated.\n  `injective_code_can_beat_clog` exhibits an injective code of `{0,1,2}` with all\n  codewords of length `\u2264 1 < 2 = \u2308log\u2082 3\u2309`.  The correct statement for injective codes is\n  `exists_codeword_length_ge`: some codeword has length at least `\u2308log\u2082(n+2)\u2309 \u2212 1`.  The\n  original bound is recovered exactly when `n + 1` is a power of two \u2014 which is the case\n  for the tower families, whence conjecture 2 above.\n\n## Open directions suggested by this development\n\n1. **Prefix-free Kraft bound.** Prove the `\u2308log\u2082(n+1)\u2309` bound for prefix-free codes on an\n   arbitrary interval `[0, n]` (Kraft's inequality is not yet available here).  This would\n   restore the last clause of conjecture 5 in its intended form.\n2. **General polynomial radices.** Extend `sqPos_not_bigO_L2` from `V\u2096 = 2^(2^k)` to every\n   monotone `r` with `r x \u2264 x^C`.\n3. **Exact difference `K \u2212 L\u2082`.** The data suggest `K \u2212 L\u2082 \u2208 {\u22121, 0, 1, 2}` with `2`\n   attained only at `n = 2` and `1` only at `n \u2208 {1, 3}`.  A complete classification of the\n   fibres of `K \u2212 L\u2082` looks within reach of the same techniques.\n4. **A balanced analogue of the compression bound.** The balanced tower digits of\n   section 4 should admit an optimal fixed-width code as in `blockCode_optimal`, with\n   `\u2308log\u2082 r\u1d62\u2309 = W\u1d62 + 1` bits per digit; the small loss `\u2211 1 = k` bits relative to the\n   information-theoretic optimum would then be exactly quantified.\n5. **Zeckendorf-side digit uniqueness.** `Zidx` is defined here as the largest Fibonacci\n   index below `n`; formalising the full Zeckendorf expansion (existence and uniqueness of\n   the non-consecutive representation) would let the comparison in section 5 be stated\n   directly in terms of the expansion rather than its top index.\n",
