    "description": "# Future directions \u2014 strongly complete sets (thread th_20f13f16, cycle 2)\n\nThis cycle added two files to the catalog:\n\n* `Catalog/Combinatorics/StronglyCompleteBlocks.lean` \u2014 monotonicity, the initial-segment\n  criterion (`StronglyComplete A \u2194 \u2200 k, Complete (A \u2229 (k, \u221e))`), the **ordered-block\n  criterion** `stronglyComplete_of_orderedBlocks`, its dyadic specialization, and the\n  sharp negative result `six_per_block_insufficient`.\n* `Catalog/Combinatorics/StronglyCompleteResidues.lean` \u2014 the **backbone-and-residues\n  criterion**, the dilation principle, the refutation of the parity conjecture, the\n  necessary congruence condition for strong completeness, and the arithmetic\n  characterization `tsum_sqDist_top_iff` of the analytic divergence hypothesis at the\n  rational test points `1/d`.\n\nAll statements are proved with no `sorry` and no extra axioms.\n\n## What survived, what failed\n\n* **Survived.** Every combinatorial criterion here needs *two* ingredients that are stable\n  under finite deletion: a size mechanism (blocks whose subset sums cover long intervals,\n  or a backbone representing all large multiples of `d`) and a congruence mechanism\n  (infinitely many elements in each residue class). Each of the two alone provably fails:\n  `multiplesOfThree` has unbounded size but no residues, `evenWithOne` has a residue but\n  only finitely often.\n* **Failed as stated.** \"Complete + infinitely many odd elements \u27f9 strongly complete\" is\n  false (`complete_infinite_odd_not_stronglyComplete`); parity is only the `d = 2` instance\n  of an obstruction that exists for every modulus.\n* **Needs a different definition.** A purely combinatorial \"six elements per dyadic block\"\n  criterion cannot exist: `six_per_block_insufficient` produces sets with arbitrarily many\n  elements per block that are not even complete. Any such criterion must import a\n  hypothesis (analytic or congruential) that excludes subgroup obstructions.\n\n## Conjectures for the next cycle\n\n### C1 (Residue-rich completeness). *If `A` is complete and, for every `m \u2265 2` and every `r`, the class `r mod m` contains infinitely many elements of `A`, then `A` is strongly complete.*\n\nThe key insight is that after deleting a finite `F`, the two failure modes are a\n*congruence* failure and a *size* failure; residue richness kills the first outright, and\ncompleteness of `A` forces `\u2211_{a \u2264 x, a \u2208 A} a \u2265 x - O(1)`, a size condition that a finite\ndeletion changes only by a constant. Why now? This cycle proved the conjecture whenever `A`\ncarries a `d\u2115`-backbone (`stronglyComplete_of_backbone_and_residues`), so only the\nbackbone-free case remains, and the necessary condition\n`infinite_not_dvd_of_stronglyComplete` shows the residue hypothesis is exactly of the right\ntype. Testable: either extend the backbone argument by replacing \"multiples of `d`\" with\n\"subset sums of a tail of `A`\", or construct a complete, residue-rich set whose\ncompleteness depends on one small element.\n\n### C2 (Quantitative divergence upgrade). *There is an absolute constant `c > 0` such that: if `A` has at least five elements in every sufficiently large dyadic block and `\u2211_{a \u2208 A} \u2016a\u03b8\u2016\u00b2 \u2265 c \u00b7 log X` for every non-integral `\u03b8` and every `X` (sum restricted to `a \u2264 X`), then `A` is strongly complete.*\n\nThe key insight is that the block criterion proved here needs the subset sums of one block\ngroup to cover an interval, and a quantitative divergence bound is exactly what converts\n\"five well-spread elements per scale\" into interval coverage after `O(1)` scales. Why now?\n`tsum_sqDist_top_iff` shows that plain divergence only encodes the qualitative statement\n\"infinitely many elements outside every `d\u2115`\", which by `six_per_block_insufficient` cannot\nsuffice on its own; the quantitative form is the first hypothesis strong enough to have a\nchance.\n\n### C3 (Deletion depth is unbounded). *For every `k` there is a set `A_k` that stays complete after deleting any `k` elements but fails to be complete after some deletion of `k + 1` elements.*\n\nThe key insight is that `threeAndUnits` (deletion depth `1`: deleting `{1,2}` kills it,\ndeleting either alone does not) should generalize by stacking independent congruence\nobstructions modulo distinct primes `p_1 < \u2026 < p_{k+1}`. Why now? The refutation machinery\nof this cycle (`infinite_not_dvd_of_stronglyComplete` plus explicit witnesses) is exactly\nthe tool needed, and a positive answer would show that \"strongly complete\" is not the top\nof a finite hierarchy but the limit of a strictly increasing one.\n\n### C4 (Rational divergence is not enough). *There is a set `A` satisfying the rational instances of the divergence hypothesis \u2014 `\u2211_{a \u2208 A} \u2016a p/q\u2016\u00b2 = \u221e` for every non-integral rational `p/q` \u2014 with six elements in every large dyadic block, which is not complete.*\n\nThe key insight is that `tsum_sqDist_top_iff` translates the rational instances into a\npurely congruential statement, so a set that is residue-rich but lacunary in size (blocks\nof six elements placed at multiplicatively spread positions) should satisfy them while\nfailing the size requirement. Why now? Such a witness would prove that the *irrational*\ntest points in the paper's hypothesis are indispensable, delimiting exactly how much of the\nanalytic input is arithmetic in disguise.\n\n### C5 (Structure of the deletion ideal). *For a complete `A`, the family `I(A) = {F finite : A \\ F is not complete}` is upward closed and generated by finitely many minimal elements iff `A` fails only finitely many congruence conditions.*\n\nThe key insight is that each minimal `F` should correspond to a modulus `d` for which\n`{a \u2208 A : d \u2224 a}` is finite, so counting minimal obstructions is counting bad moduli. Why\nnow? `infinite_not_dvd_of_stronglyComplete` gives the easy direction (no bad modulus is\nneeded for strong completeness), and the dilation principle constructs sets with\nprescribed bad moduli, so both directions are within reach of the existing formal API.\n",
