    "description": "Let `G` be a finite connected graph with `V` vertices and `E` edges, all stalks\nequal to a fixed `d`-dimensional space over a field `K`, and let each oriented\nedge carry an invertible restriction map, so that the sheaf is a\n`GL_d(K)`-local system.  Then\n\n```\ndim H\u00b9(G; \ud835\udcd5)  =  d\u00b7(E - V) + dim (K^d)^{hol(\ud835\udcd5)},\n```\n\nwhere `hol(\ud835\udcd5) \u2264 GL_d(K)` is the holonomy group generated by transporting around\na basis of cycles at a base point, and `(K^d)^{hol}` is its fixed subspace.\nEquivalently: `dim H\u2070 = dim (K^d)^{hol}` and the Euler characteristic\n`euler_characteristic` does the rest.\n\n*The key insight is* that for invertible restriction maps the entire cohomology\nof a connected nerve is controlled by a single group-theoretic invariant \u2014 the\nfixed space of the holonomy representation \u2014 with the combinatorics entering\nonly through the cycle rank `E - V + 1`.  The proved cases\n`cyclic_holonomy_criterion` (`d = 1`, `G` a cycle: fixed space is `K` iff\n`\u220f a\u1d62 = 1`) and `finrank_H1_disjointLoops` (a disconnected instance) are the two\nextreme corners of this formula.\n\n*Why now?*  The two proved corners already exhibit both terms of the right-hand\nside separately, and the abstract ledger `euler_characteristic` /\n`finrank_H1_add_finrank_range` is in place, so only the identification\n`H\u2070 = (K^d)^{hol}` remains.  Falsifiable by enumerating `2 \u00d7 2` matrices over\n`\ud835\udd3d\u2083` on the theta graph.",
