    "description": "**Conjecture.** For block sorting, define the *garbage index* of a reversible protocol as\n`|Aux| / (m!\u00b7n!)`.  Then the minimal number of elementary uncomputation steps needed to\nbring a protocol to the garbage-free case is `\u0398(log (garbage index))`, and the class of\nprotocols with garbage index `1` is exactly the class characterised by\n`block_history_equality_iff_no_garbage` \u2014 i.e. bijectivity of the history map is not only\nnecessary and sufficient but *quantitatively stable*: a protocol within a factor `1 + \u03b5` of\nthe minimum has history map surjective on a `(1 \u2212 \u03b5)`-fraction of `Aux`.\n\nThe key insight is that our equality theorem is an all-or-nothing pigeonhole statement, while\n`block_history_strict_of_garbage` shows the failure mode is a cardinality defect that should\ncarry a metric, not just a boolean. **Why now?** Both endpoints (equality iff bijective, and\nstrictness under non-surjectivity) are proved, so the interpolating stability statement is\nthe only missing piece and is falsifiable on `(m, n) = (3, 2)` by exhaustive search.",
