    "description": "**Conjecture.** Let `A(n, q)` be the set of transcript maps realisable by *adaptive\ncomparison* algorithms, where the `k`-th query is a function of the previous answers and is\none of the `q`-outcome order queries on `n` items.  Then `A(n, q)` is strictly smaller than\nthe set of injective maps `Perm (Fin n) \u2192 Fin d \u2192 Fin q` for every `q \u2265 3` and every\n`d < \u2308log_q (n!)\u2309 + c` with an absolute constant `c \u2265 1`, and the exact minimal adaptive\ndepth exceeds the counting bound `\u2308log_q (n!)\u2309` for infinitely many `(n, q)`.\n\nThe key insight is that our proved lower bound `Sorter.clog_le_depth` uses only injectivity,\nwhereas realisable transcripts must additionally be *consistent*: the fibre of any prefix of\na transcript is an order ideal cut out by the queries asked so far, and multiway order\nqueries cannot split a permutation class into `q` arbitrary pieces. **Why now?** The counting\nbound is proved and its achievability (`exists_sorter_clog_depth`) is proved to hold in the\n*abstract* transcript model, so the gap between the two models is now a sharply posed,\nfinitely testable question for small `(n, q)`.",
