    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "For a positive-definite `Q` on `\u2124\u207f` the covering radius\u00b2 is the maximum, over the Delaunay cells\n`D` of the lattice, of the circumradius\u00b2 of `D`, and this maximum is a rational function of the\nGram matrix with denominator a power of `det Q`.  In rank two the formula is `covRad`, whose\ndenominator is `4 det Q`; the conjecture is that in rank `n` the denominator divides\n`(2 det Q)^{n-1}`.\n\n*The key insight is* that cycle 11 proves the rank-two case by two entirely separate mechanisms \u2014\nan *integral* inequality `a p(p\u22121) + c q(q\u22121) + b p q \u2265 0` for the lower bound, and the *Lagrange\nbarycentric identity* plus concavity for the upper bound \u2014 and neither mechanism refers to the\nrank: the barycentric identity holds in any rank, and the integral inequality is the statement\nthat the recentred paraboloid `Q(x) \u2212 \u2113(x)` is nonnegative on the lattice, where `\u2113` is the linear\nform dual to the deep hole.\n\n*Why now?* `covering_data_of_basis` shows the whole argument is stable under `GL_n(\u2124)`, so only a\n*combinatorial* input is missing \u2014 a nonnegative-weight decomposition of the fundamental cell into\nsimplices with lattice vertices, i.e. an obtuse superbasis, which exists in rank `\u2264 3`.  Rank\nthree is therefore an immediate, finite target.\n\n---",
