    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "*(The exponential case is now closed: `geometric_scaling_law` proves\n`b(m+1)/2 \u2264 noiseFloor \u2264 b(m+1) + b/(1-r)` at the natural cut\n`r^{m+1} \u2264 b \u2264 r^m`, i.e. `noiseFloor \u224d b\u00b7log(1/b)/log(1/r)`.  What remains is\nthe power-law case, where the counting function is polynomial rather than\nlogarithmic.)*\n\nFor the power-law spectrum `a\u1d62 = (i+1)^{-\u03b2}` with `\u03b2 > 1`, truncated at any\n`n \u2265 b^{-1/\u03b2}`, there are constants `0 < c_\u03b2 \u2264 C_\u03b2` with\n\n```\nc_\u03b2 \u00b7 b^{1 - 1/\u03b2}  \u2264  noiseFloor(a, b)  \u2264  C_\u03b2 \u00b7 b^{1 - 1/\u03b2}.\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that the head/tail sandwich of Part IV converts the\nanalytic scaling law into pure counting: `#{i : a\u1d62 \u2265 b}` plus a tail energy, so\nthe exponent `1 - 1/\u03b2` is a statement about the *counting function of the\nspectrum*, not about any optimisation.\n\n**Why now?** `half_minSum_le_noiseFloor` and `minSum_head_tail` reduce the\nproblem to two elementary sums, both of which have closed forms (geometric) or\nstandard integral comparisons (power law); this would give the first fully\nformal derivation of a neural-scaling-law exponent from a variational principle.\n\n**Falsifier:** a `\u03b2` for which the two-sided bound fails for arbitrarily small\n`b`.\n\n---",
