    "description": "**Conjecture.** Shor's period-finding applied to `k \u21a6 a^k mod N` recovers\n`ord_N(a)` in polynomial time but *provably cannot* recover any individual\n`ord_p(a)`: the reduced-modulus data `{ord_{N/d}(a)}` is accessible to a quantum\nsampler only through states supported on the strata `S_d`, whose total weight is\n`(p+q\u22121)/N = O(N^{-1/2})` for balanced semiprimes. Formally: preparing a\nuniform superposition over a non-unit stratum requires a factor of `N`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the asymmetry proved here lives entirely on a set of\nmeasure `O(N^{-1/2})`, so the quantum advantage in period finding does not\ntransfer to the asymmetric readout without a state-preparation oracle that is\nitself as hard as factoring.\n\n*Why now?* The density statements are proved (`informative_density`,\n`informative_density_sqrt`), and the stratum decomposition is available as a\nLean-level partition (`sum_stratum_card`), which is exactly the decomposition a\nquantum register would need.",
