    "description": "**Conjecture.** For `N = p\u2081 \u22ef p_k` with independent uniform classes in a finite group `G` of order\n`n`, the accessible dial satisfies\n```\nI(class N ; [some p\u1d62 splits]) \u2264 k\u00b2 / n\u00b2   for all k \u2265 2, n \u2265 2,\n```\nand, for fixed `n`, `I` is strictly decreasing in `k` with at least geometric rate: extra factors\n*destroy* the dial rather than accumulate it (the measured ratios `I(k+1)/I(k)` are `\u2248 1/7` at\n`n = 3` and `\u2248 1/16` at `n = 4`).\n\n**The key insight is** that the product map `G\u1d4f \u2192 G` is a homomorphism with uniform fibres, so the\nconditional law of the split pattern given `class N` depends on the observable only through the\nevent `class N = 1`; inclusion\u2013exclusion over the `k` factors then makes the fibre discrepancy\n`O(k (n\u22121)^{k\u22121} / n^k)`, and squaring it inside the `\u03c7\u00b2` bound is what produces the rapid decay\nin `k`.\n**Why now?** The wall half is proved for all `k` (`kfactor_wall`), the `k = 2` dial is exact with a\nsharp constant (C6), and the `\u03c7\u00b2` bound `mutualInfo_le_chi2` converts a fibre count directly into\nan information bound; the missing ingredient is purely combinatorial.  Falsifiable by one\n`(n, k)` with a measured dial above `k\u00b2/n\u00b2`; the measured table (`ComputationalEvidence.md`,\ncycle 2) is consistent and suggests the bound is far from tight.",
