    "description": "**Conjecture.**  Let `S` be a finite candidate set and `h` a hint.  Define the\ninvariance group `\u0393_h = { \u03c3 \u2208 Sym(S) : h \u2218 \u03c3 = h }`.  Then the number of\nreadings satisfies `#readings = |S| / |\u0393_h|` **iff** `\u0393_h` acts freely and\ntransitively on every class; and in general `#readings \u00b7 min_orbit \u2264 |S|`, with\nequality exactly when all orbits are of equal size.  Cycle III proves the\ninequality (`card_image_mul_le`); the conjecture is the equality case and its\ncharacterisation.\n\n*The key insight is* that a hint is not a function but a partition, and a\npartition with a group of symmetries can only be as fine as the coarsest orbit\ndecomposition \u2014 so the \"wasted bits\" of every hint family in the taxonomy are a\nsingle group-theoretic invariant, not four separate accidents.\n\n*Why now?*  Cycles I\u2013III already give the two extreme cases in Lean (trivial\ngroup: exact `2^t`; Klein four-group: exact `2^{t-3}`), so the general statement\ncan be attacked with the existing `cost_ge_of_family` / `card_image_mul_le`\nmachinery plus Mathlib's `MulAction` orbit-counting API.",
