    "description": "Cycle 2 proved the qualitative half of the original Conjecture D\n(`integral_H1_torsion`, `torsion_obstruction_invisible_to_field_coefficients`).\nThe sharpened form now conjectures the exact module, and extends it off the\ndisjoint-loop nerve.  For stalks `\u2124` and restriction scalars `a\u1d62 \u2208 \u2124`:\n\n```\nH\u00b9(disjoint loops)  \u2245  \u2a01\u1d62 \u2124/(a\u1d62 - 1)\u2124,\nH\u00b9(the n-cycle)     \u2245  \u2124/(\u220f\u1d62 a\u1d62 - 1)\u2124.\n```\n\nSo on a connected cyclic nerve the *entire* integral obstruction is the cyclic\ngroup cut out by the holonomy minus one, and the field-valued monodromy\ncriterion `cyclic_holonomy_criterion` is its reduction modulo the statement\n\"is this group infinite?\".\n\n*The key insight is* that the exponent of the integral obstruction is a\nholonomy invariant, not a rank invariant: `\u220f\u1d62 a\u1d62 - 1` refines the field-valued\ndichotomy `\u220f\u1d62 a\u1d62 = 1` vs `\u2260 1` into an infinite arithmetic hierarchy, so two\ndatasets that look identical to every rational computation can be separated by\na single congruence.\n\nCycle 3 already proves the *exponent* half of this (the determinant of the\ncyclic coboundary is `\u00b1(\u220f\u1d62a\u1d62 - 1)`, and `det_smul_H1Zmat_eq_zero` then kills the\nmodule); what remains is that the module is *cyclic*, i.e. the Smith normal form\nhas a single nontrivial invariant factor.\n\n*Why now?*  `ker_transport` and `mem_range_loopDZ` already supply both halves of\nthe argument \u2014 transport identifies the cocycle condition and the divisibility\ndescription identifies the coboundary image \u2014 so the cyclic case is a Smith\nnormal form computation on an explicitly triangular matrix.  Falsifiable by\ncomputing `H\u00b9` of the `\u2124`-sheaf on the `2`-cycle with `a = (2,2)`: the\nconjecture predicts `\u2124/3`.",
