    "description": "**Conjecture.** For every complex square matrix `A`, `det (exp A) = exp (tr A)`;\nconsequently a Hermitian `H` satisfies `exp (i H) \u2208 SU(n)` if and only if\n`tr H \u2208 2\u03c0 \u2124`, which is the exact trace congruence class asked for in\ndirection 5.\n\n*The key insight is* that the missing ingredient is not the determinant\nidentity itself but a **norm-independence lemma for the exponential on a\nfinite-dimensional algebra**: the matrix exponential is usually developed\nagainst the `L^\u221e` operator norm, while the C\u22c6-structure \u2014 and hence the\ncontinuous functional calculus used for exponential surjectivity \u2014 lives on the\n`L^2` operator norm. The two exponentials are the same function but are not the\nsame object as written. Establishing that the exponential depends only on the\ntopological ring structure unlocks both frameworks simultaneously.\n\n*Why now?* This cycle produced the first statement that mixes the two worlds \u2014\nthe matrix corollary of exponential surjectivity is stated in the `L\u00b2` setting \u2014\nso the mismatch is now an explicit, isolated, provable lemma rather than a\ndiffuse inconvenience.",
