    "description": "**Conjecture.** For a cyclic order `n` with at least three splitting types, *every*\nbinary read-out `h \u2218 T` satisfies `H(h \u2218 T) < H(T) \u2212 \u03b4(n)` with an explicit\n`\u03b4(n) > 0` depending only on the `\u03c6`-rates; in particular no binary read-out of a\nmulti-state type channel can reach the type entropy.\n\n*Status.* The non-strict inequality is a theorem for all read-outs (`uEnt_comp_le`);\nstrictness is proved only for the two natural read-outs at `n = 4, 6`\n(`rootCount_lossy_4/6`, `Isplit_lt_Ipair_4/6`).\n\n*The key insight is* that the entropy defect of a coarsening equals the average\nentropy *inside* the merged fibres, which for a binary partition of a `k`-state\n`\u03c6`-law is bounded below by the smallest two rates.\n\n*Why now?* The proof of `uEnt_comp_le` is already term-wise; converting one\ninequality in that proof into a strict one on a single fibre gives the result.",
