    "description": "The original conjecture \u2014 that the completeness threshold is `\u0398(N)` rather than `\u0398(\u221aN)` \u2014 is now a\ntheorem: the least positive exponent with `R_k(N) = \u03c6(N)` is exactly\n`lcm(p-1, q-1) = \u03c6(N)/gcd(p-1, q-1)`, and this extends to every squarefree modulus, where the\nthreshold is the Carmichael exponent `lcm_{r | N}(r-1)`.\n\nA later cycle went further: `R_k(N) = \u220f_{p | N} gcd(\u03c6(p^{v_p(N)}), k)` for *every* odd `N`, which\nidentifies the aggregation depth of an arbitrary odd modulus as its Carmichael exponent\n`\u03bb(N) = lcm_{p | N} \u03c6(p^{v_p(N)})`.\n\n**Successor conjecture (the 2-adic case).** For `N = 2^e \u00b7 M` with `M` odd and `e \u2265 3`,\n`R_k(N) = gcd(2, k) \u00b7 gcd(2^{e-2}, k) \u00b7 \u220f_{p | M} gcd(\u03c6(p^{v_p}), k)`, and the least complete\nexponent is `lcm(2^{e-2}, \u03bb(M))` \u2014 strictly smaller than `\u03c6(N)`, so the even part *shortens* the\naggregation depth by a factor of two without ever making it polynomial.\n\n*The key insight is* that the proof for odd moduli needed only multiplicativity of the witness over\ncoprime factors plus cyclicity of each local unit group, and cyclicity fails exactly at `2^e`,\n`e \u2265 3`, where the local group is `Z/2 \u00d7 Z/2^{e-2}`; the missing ingredient is a root count in a\nproduct of two cyclic groups, which the multiplicativity of root counts over direct products already\nsupplies.\n\n*Why now?* Multiplicativity of the witness over coprime factors and the product root count are both\nestablished, and the `Z/2 \u00d7 Z/2^{e-2}` structure of the unit group modulo `2^e` is standard, so only\nthe transport step is missing.",
