    "description": "**Conjecture.** For every polynomial-time generator family `G_n : {0,1}^{s(n)} \u2192 {0,1}^n`\nand every polynomial-time decoder, the number of `n`-bit strings compressible\nbelow `n - d` bits is at most `2^{n-d+1}`, and moreover the *time-bounded*\ncomplexity `KC^t` satisfies `KC^t(x) \u2265 KC(x)` with the counting bound holding\nuniformly in `t`; hence no complexity assumption (P \u2260 NP, one-way functions)\ncan ever be used to beat pigeonhole.\n\n*The key insight is* that the counting argument never inspects the decoder, so\nit survives verbatim under any resource bound \u2014 bounding resources can only\n*remove* decodable strings, never add them.\n*Why now?* Our `KC` and `exists_shortest_code` are stated for an arbitrary\n`D : List Bool \u2192 Bits n`; specializing `D` to a step-indexed interpreter is a\npurely definitional extension of the existing files.",
