    "description": "Extend `nondetExtIter : \u2115 \u2192 SurvivalGame \u2192 SurvivalGame` to a tower indexed by ordinals: at\nsuccessors refine by `\u03c9`, at limits take the dependent lexicographic sum used to build\n`limitGame`. Conjecturally the value at level `\u03b4` is `\u03c9 ^ \u03b4` for every `\u03b4` below the first\nfixed point of `\u03b4 \u21a6 \u03c9 ^ \u03b4`, and the tower's own limit is exactly `\u03b5\u2080`:\n\n```lean\ntheorem towerValue (\u03b4 : Ordinal) : (tower \u03b4 finiteGame).value = \u03c9 ^ (\u03b4 + 1)\ntheorem tower_limit_value : (towerLimit finiteGame).value = \u03b5\u2080\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that the level-`k` bound already proved here comes entirely from the key\nfunction `limitKey \u27e8k, a\u27e9 = \u03c9 ^ k + typein a`, and that key is definable uniformly in the level:\nreplacing `k : \u2115` by `\u03b4 : Ordinal` turns the finite Cantor-normal-form argument into the general\none, so no new combinatorics is needed \u2014 only a type-level transfinite recursion.\n\n**Why now?** The `\u03a3\u2097`-based limit construction is verified, including its well-foundedness\n(the actual obstacle: Mathlib has no `IsWellOrder` instance for lexicographic sigma types, and\nthe key function supplies one). Moreover `ImmortalitySigmaLimit.sigmaGame_value_eq` already\nproves the *general* limit step \u2014 any chain of lives cofinal in an additively principal ordinal\nattains it \u2014 so only the transfinite type-level recursion remains to be built.\n\n---",
