    "description": "**Conjecture.** The sequence `n \u21a6 n\u00b2 I(n)` is strictly decreasing for `n \u2265 2` and\n`n\u00b2 I(n) > 1 \u2212 log 2` for every `n`, with the sharp two-sided expansion\n```\nn\u00b2 I(n) = (1 \u2212 log 2) + 1/(4n) + O(1/n\u00b2) .\n```\n(The tabulated differences `n\u00b7(n\u00b2I(n) \u2212 (1 \u2212 log 2))` are `0.253` at `n = 100` and `0.250` at\n`n = 1000`, so the first-order coefficient is `1/4`, not the `9/4` of the polynomial part alone:\nthe cubic Taylor terms contribute `\u22122` to it.)\n\n**The key insight is** that the `1/n` coefficient is a *cancellation residue* \u2014 the `9/4` produced\nby the quadratic Taylor part is cut down to `1/4` by the cubic parts of the three logarithms \u2014 so\nmonotonicity of the dial is equivalent to positivity of a single explicit\nrational-plus-logarithmic function of `n`, with no group theory left in it.\n**Why now?** The exact identity `n_sq_semiprimeDial_eq` reduces everything to a one-variable\ninequality, and the Taylor machinery (`abs_log_one_sub_taylor_le`) is already in place; only a\nderivative sign is missing.\nFalsifiable by a single pair `n < m` with `n\u00b2 I(n) < m\u00b2 I(m)`; the tabulated values\n`0.8630, 0.4540, 0.3979, 0.3352, 0.3094, \u2026` are consistent with the claim.",
