    "description": "Let `A_S = {a : \u03a3 a\u1d62 \u2264 S}`.  Then the *regret of the best single ridge parameter*\nagainst the Wiener optimum, in the worst case over `A_S`, is governed by the\ncapacity gap:\n\n```\nsup_{a \u2208 A_S}  [ inf_\u03bb filterRisk(a,b,ridge \u03bb) - noiseFloor(a,b) ]\n      \u224d  b \u00b7 ( logDet(a*, b) - effDim(a*, b) )   for the maximising a*.\n```\n\n**The key insight is** that both the ridge deficiency and the capacity gap\nmeasure the same thing \u2014 the spread of the numbers `a\u1d62/(a\u1d62+b)` around their mean\n\u2014 so an inequality between two symmetric concave functionals should be provable\nby the same tangent-line technique used in `noiseFloor_le_flat`.\n\n**Why now?** Round 6 supplies all three quantities in formal form (`filterRisk`\nfor ridge, `noiseFloor`, `logDet`) plus the tangent-line machinery\n(`tangent_identity`); the conjecture is the first quantitative link between\ninformation-theoretic capacity and the *suboptimality of a hyperparameter\nfamily*, rather than of an estimator.\n\n**Falsifier:** a spectrum with a large capacity gap but ridge-optimal risk (or\nvice versa), e.g. found by optimising over `Fin 3` spectra numerically.\n\n---",
