    "description": "**Conjecture.** For a boundary-dependent seed, the `zeroN` retention decays like\n`base/(gain\u00b7k)`; equivalently `k \u00b7 retention(k)` converges and the retention\nseries over widths diverges (`retention_asymptotic`, `not_summable_retention`).\nA single positive-width read therefore determines the whole profile\n(`retention_profile_determined`).\n\n*The key insight is* that if the answer path mixes a width-independent internal\ndrive with `k` equal boundary contributions, then retention is a M\u00f6bius\n(fractional-linear) function of `k`, and M\u00f6bius functions are rigid: one value\nplus the fixed point at `k = 0` pins the rest.\n\n*Why now?* The round's own numbers already put this under stress: fitting\n`gain/base` at `k = 2` predicts `k = 3` retentions of `0.672 / 0.877 / 0.732 /\n0.866` against measured `0.704 / 0.901 / 0.710 / 0.744` \u2014 good for seeds\n`13, 14, 15`, **badly off for seed `17`** (see `ComputationalEvidence.md`).\n**Test:** measure `k = 4` for seeds `15` and `17`. Harmonic decay predicts\nretention `\u2248 0.65` for `s = 15`; a second boundary channel switching on at\n`k = 3` (the natural repair for `s = 17`) predicts a visible kink instead.\n\n---",
